### Q48. Synchronous Generator Short-Circuit Current

**Question:** A synchronous generator has emf = 11kV, synchronous reactance = 10Ω/phase, armature resistance negligible. The short-circuit current for a field current producing rated voltage on open circuit is:

(a) 1100 A  
(b) 1000 A  
(c) 800 A  
(d) 600 A

**Answer: (d) 600 A** *(closest to the computed 635 A)*

> **[KEY-FIXED]** Original key read "(a) 1100 A". That value comes from dividing the **line** voltage by a **per-phase** reactance, which mixes units. Corrected below.

**Solution:**

Because \( X_s \) is quoted **per phase**, the emf must also be taken per phase. For an 11 kV (line) machine:
\[
E_{ph} = \frac{11\,000}{\sqrt{3}} = 6351\ \text{V}
\]
\[
I_{sc} = \frac{E_{ph}}{X_s} = \frac{6351}{10} = 635.1\ \text{A}
\]

Nearest option is **(d) 600 A**.

**Why 1100 A is wrong:** \( 11\,000/10 = 1100 \) A divides a line quantity by a phase quantity. It is only correct for a **single-phase** machine, or if the stem had quoted a per-phase emf of 11 kV.

**Alternative Questions:**
1. What is the short-circuit current if Xs is doubled? **(635/2 ≈ 318 A)**
2. What is the line current for this fault? **(Same value — the fault current is the per-phase current, 635 A)**

