### Q34. DC Motor Back EMF and Torque

**Question:** A DC motor (separately excited) has armature resistance \( R_a = 0.2Ω \), applied voltage V = 240V, and armature current \( I_a = 100A \) at rated load. Armature reaction and friction are negligible. Find back-emf \( E_b \) and developed torque if speed N = 1200rpm. (Use \( P_{mech} = E_b I_a \) and torque \( T = P_{mech}/\omega \)).

**Answer: none — the correct values are \( E_b = 220\ V,\ T \approx 175\ N \cdot m \)**

> **[BROKEN SOURCE]** Original key read "(b) \( E_b = 220\ V,\ T = 349.1\ N\cdot m \)". \( E_b \) is right, but \( T = P_{mech}/\omega = 22000/125.66 = 175 \) N·m, which matches no option. Kept for audit; **not a valid MCQ**.

**Solution:**

Back-emf:

\[
E_b = V - I_aR_a = 240 - (100)(0.2) = 220\ \text{V}
\]

Mechanical power and speed in rad/s:

\[
P_{mech} = E_bI_a = (220)(100) = 22000\ \text{W}
\]

\[
\omega = \frac{2\pi N}{60} = \frac{2\pi(1200)}{60} = 125.66\ \text{rad/s}
\]

Developed torque:

\[
T = \frac{P_{mech}}{\omega} = \frac{22000}{125.66} = 175.1\ \text{N}\cdot\text{m}
\]

Cross-check with the power-speed torque relation \( T = 9.55P/N \) (P in W, N in rpm): \( 9.55(22000)/1200 = 175.1 \) N·m ✓. (The option 349.1 N·m corresponds to \( 2P_{mech}/\omega \) — twice the correct developed torque.)

**Alternative Questions:**
1. What is the input power? **(240 × 100 = 24000W)**
2. What is the efficiency? **(22000/24000 = 91.67%)**

